<sn><md>### History of Computer Networks

**Overview**

Computer networks evolved from `simple remote computer communication` in the 1940s into the `modern Internet` through the development of **modems, time-sharing, packet switching, ARPANET, TCP/IP, and Ethernet**.

**Main concept:** Computer networking developed progressively from remote computer communication and resource sharing into `interconnected networks` using packet switching, internetworking protocols, and high-speed Ethernet.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Early Origins (1940–1960s)

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/computer_history_early_origin.jpeg)

| Year | Event | Description |
|------|-------|-------------|
| 1940 | `George Stibitz` | Demonstrated the `first real-time remote use of a computer` by connecting a *teletype* at Dartmouth to a Bell Labs computer. |
| 1950s | SAGE | The U.S. military developed a computer network for its radar system using the Bell 101 modem. |
| 1958 | `Bell 101 Modem` | `AT&amp;T` released the *first commercial computer modem*, transmitting digital data over telephone lines at `110 bits` per second. |
| 1959 | Time-sharing | Christopher Strachey and John McCarthy contributed to the development of time-sharing, allowing `multiple users to share computing resources`. |
| 1960s | Packet switching | Paul Baran and Donald Davies independently developed the concept of packet switching for computer data communication. |
| 1968–1969 | NPL Network | The National Physical Laboratory implemented packet switching in a local area network using `768 kbit/s `links. |

**Time-Sharing (1959):** A technique where `multiple users share one computer simultaneously`. The CPU rapidly switches between users' tasks, giving each the illusion of dedicated access. It made computing interactive and cost-effective.

**Packet Switching (1960s):** A method of `sending data by breaking it into small packets` that travel independently across a network and are reassembled at the destination. It is efficient, resilient, and the foundation of the Internet.</md></sn><sn><md>### Important Dates for Exams

